In Python, functions are created using the def keyword.
def &lt;function_name&gt;(&lt;argument&gt;):
    &lt;code_of_the_function&gt;
The def keyword marks the beginning of a function definition.
The function name comes after def.
The code inside the function must be indented.

The else statement provides an alternative. Its block executes when the if condition is False.
if &lt;condition&gt;:
    &lt;code to run if the condition is True&gt;
else:
    &lt;code to run if the condition is False&gt;
Only one of the two branches executes.
&lt;this instruction always runs&gt;

if &lt;condition&gt;:
    &lt;one branch runs&gt;
else:
    &lt;the other branch runs&gt;

&lt;this instruction always runs&gt;

# Check whether a student passed an exam
# Assume that the mark is between 0 and 10
grade = 7

if grade &gt;= 5:
    print("You passed")
else:
